WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.375
This shop runs on Azure.

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:03.775
Start at the gateway.

00:00:03.775 --> 00:00:08.400
It sends requests to the API, and so does
the web service below it.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.050
Follow either path to the right

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.675
and you’ll reach pg-shop, the shop’s
database server.

00:00:14.058 --> 00:00:16.208
How did Sattle draw those arrows?

00:00:16.208 --> 00:00:18.633
Kubernetes lists the services.

00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:21.008
Network records show who called whom.

00:00:21.008 --> 00:00:22.983
Azure lists the database server,

00:00:22.983 --> 00:00:25.733
and Azure Monitor tells us whether it
answered.

00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:30.192
Sattle puts those records together in a
map called the master graph.

00:00:30.192 --> 00:00:34.192
One query finds the parts of the shop and
the links between them.

00:00:34.192 --> 00:00:38.067
A second query uses your rules to work out
which parts are healthy.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.950
The replay comes with a working model.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.475
For your own shop, give Sattle read access

00:00:43.475 --> 00:00:46.175
to your sources and tell it where to look.

00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:51.100
Here, that’s one Kubernetes cluster and
the services in its shop namespace.

00:00:51.475 --> 00:00:54.800
You decide when to raise an alarm and when
to clear it.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.075
For pg-shop, one bad minute trips the
database alarm.

00:00:59.075 --> 00:01:01.875
It takes two good minutes in a row to
clear it.

00:01:01.875 --> 00:01:04.850
The same rule covers other servers of this
type.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.600
You also know which calls your service can
do without.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.125
If the API needs the database to answer a
request,

00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:14.950
its health should reflect a database
outage.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.625
If it can work without a cache,

00:01:16.625 --> 00:01:19.500
you can leave that call out of the health
calculation.

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:23.067
Azure inventory names pg-shop.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:26.017
The meter reading carries the same
resource ID,

00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:29.267
so Sattle can attach both records to one
server.

00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:33.117
The Kubernetes service called database has
its own ID.

00:01:33.117 --> 00:01:36.267
An arrow connects the service to the
server it uses.

00:01:36.617 --> 00:01:41.117
The white paper calls the names for things
and their links an ontology.

00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:43.567
A projection gives you a simpler map.

00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:46.967
Several copies of the web service call the
API.

00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:49.917
On the shop map those calls become one
arrow,

00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:52.067
and you can trace it back to the records.

00:01:52.475 --> 00:01:55.450
Each minute adds a reading to the database
chart.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.100
Most minutes leave the map just as it was.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.825
When a reading trips or clears the alarm,

00:02:00.825 --> 00:02:04.250
Sattle records the change in a new version
of the health map.

00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:08.392
At 10:11 the database meter drops to zero.

00:02:08.392 --> 00:02:11.892
The recorder gets that sample a minute and
a second later.

00:02:11.892 --> 00:02:15.492
The alarm belongs to 10:11, the minute on
the chart.

00:02:15.892 --> 00:02:19.567
Start at pg-shop and follow the dots back
to the left.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:22.167
The database service needs that server.

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:25.367
The API needs the service, and both the
gateway

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.992
and the web service use the API.

00:02:27.992 --> 00:02:30.342
The cache and storage stay healthy.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.550
At 10:14 the database answers again.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.300
The alarm stays on.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.375
At 10:15 we have two good minutes in a
row.

00:02:38.375 --> 00:02:40.725
The alarm clears, and the services that

00:02:40.725 --> 00:02:43.400
depend on the database turn green again.

00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:47.908
Let’s go back a few minutes, to when the
cache was under heavy load.

00:02:47.908 --> 00:02:51.358
In the original replay the web service
uses the cache.

00:02:51.358 --> 00:02:54.183
The shop and its web service show
Degraded.

00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:56.933
The API and gateway stay healthy.

00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:00.283
Now replay the same minutes with one
change.

00:03:00.283 --> 00:03:03.458
We put the cache address in the API’s
settings.

00:03:03.458 --> 00:03:06.258
Sattle finds that address in Azure
inventory

00:03:06.258 --> 00:03:09.683
and draws a new arrow from the API to the
cache.

00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:12.683
Sattle keeps the setting as evidence for
that arrow.

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:16.933
Pause both runs at 10:06:30.

00:03:16.933 --> 00:03:21.483
In the second run the API and its gateway
both show Degraded.

00:03:21.483 --> 00:03:24.633
The cache readings and alarm rules are the
same.

00:03:24.633 --> 00:03:28.008
The gateway now depends on the cache
through the API.

00:03:28.642 --> 00:03:33.017
This time, point the API at a new cache
called offers-cache.

00:03:33.017 --> 00:03:36.592
So far, all Sattle has is an address in a
setting.

00:03:36.592 --> 00:03:41.442
Until Azure reports a matching resource,
the map has no cache at that address.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.175
Now Azure Resource Graph lists
offers-cache.

00:03:45.175 --> 00:03:48.650
Its address matches the one in the API
settings.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.750
Both caches are the same type,

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.250
so the rule that found the first one can
draw this new link too.

00:03:54.617 --> 00:03:56.917
The new cache shows up as Unknown.

00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:58.792
Azure told us it exists.

00:03:58.792 --> 00:04:02.942
To tell whether it’s healthy, Sattle needs
readings for that cache.

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:05.542
For a different kind of resource, you may
need a new rule

00:04:05.542 --> 00:04:09.567
to match its address, or a way to collect
and interpret its readings.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.100
When a source reports a change,

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.400
the engine updates the parts of the map
that depend on it.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.825
It sends those edits to the Canvas,
Sattle’s viewer.

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:22.925
If a source stops reporting, Sattle keeps
the last evidence.

00:04:22.925 --> 00:04:26.150
A quiet source can’t tell us the database
is gone.

00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:30.658
The Canvas opens a saved map and applies
the edits in order.

00:04:30.658 --> 00:04:34.908
In this recording, version 16 shows the
database outage.

00:04:34.908 --> 00:04:37.958
Skip to version 18 to see it recover.

00:04:37.958 --> 00:04:40.733
The version numbers count changes to the
map.

00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:43.508
The times on the chart belong to the
readings.

00:04:43.867 --> 00:04:45.367
The white paper explains

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:48.317
when updating the map one change at a time
gives

00:04:48.317 --> 00:04:51.692
the same answer as rebuilding it from the
beginning.

00:04:51.692 --> 00:04:54.342
Sattle tests this with a second program.

00:04:54.342 --> 00:04:56.292
Both programs get the same records

00:04:56.292 --> 00:04:59.267
and rules, and the test compares their
maps.

00:04:59.892 --> 00:05:05.367
Open the workload tree in the Canvas at
version 16 and select pg-shop.

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:09.517
The origin marker tells you the server’s
own meter tripped the alarm.

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:12.442
Select the web service and you can see why
it turned

00:05:12.442 --> 00:05:15.642
red even though the alarm came from the
database.

00:05:16.042 --> 00:05:19.442
In the Inspector, follow the health result
back to the rule,

00:05:19.442 --> 00:05:23.017
then follow the database link back to its
source records.

00:05:23.017 --> 00:05:26.042
On a running engine, you can edit a copy
of the query

00:05:26.042 --> 00:05:28.142
and preview the rows it returns.

00:05:28.492 --> 00:05:31.317
Try the two cache changes in the
walkthrough.

00:05:31.317 --> 00:05:35.667
First give the API the existing cache
address and watch the gateway.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.892
Then give it the new address and add the
Azure record.

00:05:38.892 --> 00:05:41.467
This time, look at the new cache’s health.
